<interrogationes unitas="tabula_dispersa">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make a string-keyed map and look a key up?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_creare_chorda tabula_dispersa_inserere tabula_dispersa_invenire tabula_dispersa_continet">tabula_dispersa_creare_chorda(piscina,
      capacitas) gives an open-addressing table with FNV-1a hashing and
      byte comparison; the capacity is rounded up to a power of two
      (at least XVI). Values are vacuum*. tabula_dispersa_invenire
      returns VERUM and writes *valor_out only when the key is present
      (valor_out may be NIHIL); tabula_dispersa_continet is the same
      test without the value. A stored NIHIL value is still "found",
      so test the return value, not the pointer.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I reuse or free the key's bytes after inserting it?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_inserere chorda_transcribere chorda_internare">No.
      The table stores the chorda by value, so it keeps the pointer to
      your bytes, not a copy. If those bytes are a stack buffer, are
      rolled back with piscina_reficere, or are overwritten, lookups
      quietly stop matching. Copy the key first (chorda_transcribere into
      a piscina that lives as long as the table) or intern it
      (chorda_internare).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens when I insert a key that is already in the table?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_inserere">Only the value is
      replaced. The key chorda stored the first time stays (with its
      pointer), the count does not change, and the call returns VERUM.
      Empty keys (mensura 0) are refused: inserere, invenire and delere
      all return FALSUM for them.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I look up with a C string literal instead of building a chorda?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_invenire_literis tabula_dispersa_continet_literis tabula_dispersa_creare_chorda">Yes,
      with tabula_dispersa_invenire_literis and
      tabula_dispersa_continet_literis, but only on a table made by
      tabula_dispersa_creare_chorda. The _literis path always hashes
      with FNV-1a and compares raw bytes, whatever friatio and
      comparatio the table was created with. On a table with a custom
      hash (tabula_friare_multiplicatio, or your own) it never finds
      anything.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I key a table by something other than string contents, such as pointer identity?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_creare TabulaFriatio TabulaComparatio" via="lib/insula.c">Pass
      your own TabulaFriatio and TabulaComparatio to
      tabula_dispersa_creare (it returns NIHIL if either is NIHIL). The
      comparator must return 0 for equal keys and is only called when
      the cached hashes match, so hash and equality must agree.
      lib/insula.c keys a table by the address and length of a chorda
      (friatio_sedis, comparatio_sedis).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I walk every entry, and can I change the table while iterating?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_iterator_initium tabula_dispersa_iterator_proximum tabula_dispersa_delere">tabula_dispersa_iterator_initium
      then tabula_dispersa_iterator_proximum until FALSUM; entries come
      in slot order, not insertion order (sort them yourself if the
      output must be stable). Deleting during the walk is safe:
      tabula_dispersa_delere only marks the slot as a tombstone.
      Inserting is not: an insert can rehash into a new slot array and
      the iterator, which holds only the table and a slot index, then
      skips or repeats entries; even without a rehash a new entry is
      visited or not depending on its slot.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does a table with few live entries keep using more memory as I insert and delete?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_delere tabula_dispersa_inserere tabula_dispersa_vacare tabula_dispersa_factor_deletorum">Deletion
      leaves a tombstone (SLOT_DELETUM). When live entries pass 75% of
      capacity, or tombstones pass 25%, the next insert rehashes into a
      new array of DOUBLE the capacity, even when the rehash was caused
      only by tombstones. The old array stays in the piscina (it is an
      arena), so heavy insert/delete churn grows memory with the number
      of operations, not with the live count. tabula_dispersa_vacare
      empties every slot in place without allocating.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can tabula_dispersa_inserere fail because memory ran out?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_dispersa_inserere piscina_allocare">Not
      in a way you can handle: growth allocates with piscina_allocare,
      which exits the process when memory runs out, so the "FALSUM on
      allocation failure" path in the header never actually runs.
      FALSUM in practice means a NIHIL table or an empty key.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
